UPDALL(5)

# NAME

updall - YAML schema for writing configuration.

# LOCATION

_updall_ looks for its configuration in the following locations, based on operating system:

- Windows
    - _C:\\Users\\<User>\\AppData\\Local\\updall\\config.yaml_
- Linux
    - _~/.config/updall/config.yaml_
- MacOS
    - _~/Library/Application Support/updall/config.yaml_

# SCHEMA

## Top-Level

The most basic config file would be a single key, *entries*, mapping to an empty list,
but below are all configuration options available.

[- *Key*
:- *Type*
:- *Default*
:- *Description*
|  shell
:[ _string[]_
:[ ['/bin/sh', '-c']
:< The shell used to run the _update_ and _clean_ scripts for each entry.
|  log_level
:  _0_, _1_, or _2_
:  0
:  The program's default log level. Ignored if _--verbose_/_-v_ is used.
|  entries
:  _Entry[]_
:[
:  *Required.* The list of updaters and cleaners to run in order.

## Entry

An entry is a YAML mapping consisting at minimum of a *name* and *update* key.
These are iterated on and their *update* scripts called in order of definition,
and once all entries' update scripts have run, all *clean* scripts are run
afterwords in that same order.

[- *Key*
:- *Type*
:- *Description*
|  name
:[ _string_
:< *Required.* A unique identifier string.
|  update
:  _string_
:  *Required.* A script passed as a string to _shell_.
|  clean
:  _string?_
:  An optional second script to run after all updaters.
|  when
:  _WhenCondition[]_
:  Only run this entry when *any* _WhenCondition_ resolves true.

## WhenCondition

A _WhenCondition_ resolves true when *all* of its defined mapping values are true.
Below is the list of all _WhenCondition_ keys.

[- *Key*
:- *Type*
:- *Description*
|  has_exe
:[ _string?_
:< Check if the user has a specific command on their PATH.
|  is_os
:  _string?_
:  Check if the user is on a specific OS.
|  env_equals
:  _{string: string}[]_
:  Check that all keys are evironment variables and are set to their respective value.
|  is_not
:  _WhenCondition_
:  Check that a _WhenCondition_ resolves false.

# SEE ALSO

_updall_(1)
